      "font_size": 12.07,
      "font_name": "LMRoman12-Regular",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 173,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00155_p4_b4",
      "text": "hindsight-best policy under the same plausible reward model. This benchmark is especially\n",
      "bbox": [
        72.0,
        164.33,
        523.71,
        176.16
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        288,
        657,
        2094,
        704
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 11.84,
      "font_name": "LMRoman12-Regular",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 89,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00155_p4_b5",
      "text": "natural in RLHF because one policy must be committed before the realized user preference\nis observed.\n",
      "bbox": [
        72.0,
        182.26,
        523.29,
        212.04
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        288,
        729,
        2093,
        848
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 11.84,
      "font_name": "LMRoman12-Regular",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 101,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00155_p4_b6",
      "text": "The obstacle is computational. Worst-case regret is generally much harder to optimize\n",
      "bbox": [
        89.56,
        218.11,
        523.32,
        229.94
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        358,
        872,
        2093,
        919
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 11.84,
      "font_name": "LMRoman12-Regular",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 85,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00155_p4_b7",
      "text": "than worst-case value: classical minmax-regret problems are NP-hard even in static settings\n[Averbakh, 2004, Averbakh and Lebedev, 2005], and recent ex-ante DRRO formulations\noften require substantial optimization machinery [Agarwal and Zhang, 2022, Cho and\nYang, 2024, Bitar, 2024, Fiechtner and Blanchet, 2025]. This seems discouraging for\nRLHF, where the action space is the set of all possible language-model responses.\n",
      "bbox": [
        71.57,
        236.04,
        523.57,
        319.61
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        286,
        944,
        2094,
        1278
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 11.84,
      "font_name": "LMRoman12-Regular",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 423,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00155_p4_b8",
      "text": "The main theoretical message of this paper is that promptwise RLHF has far more\nstructure than this pessimistic view suggests. For an expressive policy class, the global\n",
      "bbox": [
        72.0,
        325.49,
        523.32,
        355.48
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        288,
        1301,
        2093,
        1421
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 12.07,
      "font_name": "LMRoman12-Regular",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 169,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00155_p4_b9",
      "text": "ex-ante problem decomposes into promptwise regret problems. Each fixed-prompt problem\nbecomes a portfolio-style allocation over responses. Under Wasserstein ambiguity with\n",
      "bbox": [
        72.0,
        361.53,
        523.32,
        391.34
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        288,
        1446,
        2093,
        1565
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 11.84,
      "font_name": "LMRoman12-Regular",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 171,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00155_p4_b10",
      "text": "promptwise \u2113p transport cost, the inner adversary has an explicit dual-norm form; under\nthe \u21131 transport cost used by our algorithm, the minimizer further collapses to a water-\n",
      "bbox": [
        72.0,
        397.3,
        525.29,
        428.98
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        288,
        1589,
        2101,
        1715
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 11.92,
      "font_name": "LMRoman12-Regular",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 176,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00155_p4_b11",
      "text": "filling rule. This also explains why DRRO is less pessimistic than DRO: DRRO uses the\n",
      "bbox": [
        72.0,
        433.16,
        523.31,
        445.09
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        288,
        1732,
        2093,
        1780
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 11.93,
      "font_name": "LMRoman12-Regular",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 85,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00155_p4_b12",
      "text": "proxy\u2019s ranking information when deciding how to hedge, whereas DRO under the same\nformulation can ignore it. As ambiguity shrinks, DRRO recovers proxy-greedy behavior;\nas ambiguity grows, it converges to the uniform policy.\n",
      "bbox": [
        72.0,
        451.07,
        524.99,
        498.88
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        288,
        1804,
        2099,
